# Template for host_config.env — copy this file to host_config.env
# (gitignored, never committed) and fill in real values for your host.
#
# This repo's public interface: launch.sh sources ./host_config.env for
# structural config (which Infisical instance and project to pull the
# code-server password from — not a secret itself) and pulls the actual
# password from Infisical separately. Where host_config.env's values
# actually come from is entirely up to you — hardcode them here directly,
# or (as this operator does) generate this file from a private config
# store on your own infrastructure. This repo doesn't need to know or
# care which.
#
# launch.sh also expects a Universal Auth machine identity's bootstrap
# credential at ~/.secrets/infisical.env (untracked, never in this repo),
# defining VSCODEHOST_CLIENT_ID and VSCODEHOST_CLIENT_SECRET — and a
# secret named TOKEN_SECRET holding the code-server password, at path
# /vscode/code-server/ in the project below.

# Self-hosted Infisical instance this launch.sh pulls the password from,
# plus the project ID (UUID) /vscode/code-server/ lives in — find these
# under Project Settings -> General in your Infisical UI, or via
# `infisical init` if the UI doesn't expose it cleanly.
INFISICAL_API_URL=https://your-infisical-instance
CORE_INFRA_PROJECT_ID=your-core-infra-project-id

# Optional: a directory on the host made visible inside the container,
# read-only, at /mnt/host-share. Leave it unset and the empty ./host-share
# directory in this repo is mounted, which does nothing. Use it to hand the
# container files that another process on the host renders and that must not be
# writable from inside the container, e.g. a directory on tmpfs under /run.
# The directory must already exist when the container starts (Docker would
# otherwise create it root-owned), and its files must be readable by the
# container's `coder` user (uid 1000).
# HOST_SHARE_DIR=/run/example-share
